WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.126 --> 00:00:09.890
LOU DE LA TORRE:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.120
Welcome to the Google Cloud
Video Learning Series.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.800
My name is Lou De La Torre.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.020
I'm a customer engineer
at Google Cloud.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.300
And today I'll show
you how to export

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.810
logs from Google Cloud
Logging to BigQuery.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.330
Here I am in the
Google Cloud console.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.490
You can quickly find
the BigQuery service

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:34.800
by typing BigQuery in the Search
Products and Resources field

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.050
and then selecting BigQuery.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.470
In the Navigation pane
in the Resources section,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.550
select your project.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.410
On the right side of the
window in the Details panel,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.230
click Create Dataset.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.110
In the Create dataset
window under Dataset ID,

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:57.430
enter a unique name.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.720
In Data location, accept
the default, which is US.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.710
In Default Table Expiration,
select Number of Days

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:16.300
After Table Creation,
and I'll enter 7.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.870
Under Encryption, accept the
default Google-managed key

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.370
and select Create Dataset.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.200
Next, we'll want to turn on
audit logging for all services.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.300
By default, data
access audit logs

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.910
are disabled except
for BigQuery.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.920
In order to enable
all audit logs,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.360
we'll need to first download the
current IAM policy as a file,

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.570
then add the audit
log policy yaml object

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.540
to the current policy file,
then update our project

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.190
with the updated policy.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.500
I'll demonstrate how to
configure your project's data

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.980
access audit logs using the
gcloud command and Google Cloud

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.420
console.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.580
First, open the cloud shell
by clicking on Activate Cloud

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:04.080
Shell.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.530
Then read the project's IAM
policy and store it in a file

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.685
by running this command.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.335
Click on Authorize
Cloud Shell if prompted.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:41.170
Then edit the policy file stored
in temp/policy.yaml using VI,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.260
and add the data access
audit logs configuration.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.600
Be sure to preserve
the bindings and ETag

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.430
sections without changes.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.930
Otherwise, you might cause your
project to become unstable.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.770
Save and exit the file, then
write the new IAM policy

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.705
to the project by
running this command.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.760
Next, we'll need to refine the
logging filters to export audit

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.650
logs, including admin activity
and data access audit logs.

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.560
You could also enable logs
for specific resource types

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.190
if you like.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.520
We'll use the gcloud
logging sinks create command

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.460
to create a sink called
gcp_logging_sink_bq

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.175
for the project by
running this command.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.780
In the service account entry,
return from the API call.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.200
The identity is included
in the response.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.110
This identity represents a
Google Cloud Service account

00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:59.480
that has been created
for the export.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.550
Until you grant this
identity write access

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:05.570
to the destination, log-entry
exports from the sink

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:06.290
will fail.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.170
So let's go ahead and
copy the identity.

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.810
To add the permissions
to the service account,

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.640
select the data set.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.190
In my case, gcp_logging_export,
and select Share dataset.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.298
In the Add members
field, paste the service

00:04:35.298 --> 00:04:36.465
account that we just copied.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.400
Then select a role.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.890
Select BigQuery
and BigQuery Data

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.890
Editor to provide the
permissions necessary.

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:55.910
Click Add, and click Done.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.000
After you create the logging
export by using this filter,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.670
log files begin to populate
the BigQuery dataset

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.320
in the configured project.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.870
You can now run a broad range
of queries against the audit

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.290
logs to better understand who's
accessing data on Google Cloud,

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:14.270
or you can use a tool
like Google Data Studio

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.550
to help you visualize the data.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.290
Well, that concludes the demo.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.090
Please stay tuned
for future videos

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.770
on how to use other components
of the Google Cloud Operation

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.920
Suite as well as other services.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.330
Have a great day.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

